#Dictionaries
-comma separated key-value pairs enclosed within {}
#{key1:value1,key2:value2,.....}
uses:eg-to keep the record of groceries with value
-it does not have index
-dict are mutable

#Operation on Dictionaries
1.get()-fetch the value of keys
  syntax:dict.get(key)
 -if we use this function we dont get error if there doesn't exist any key instead it show none

2.Membership operator
-in
-it works for key

3.To update the Dictionaries
  syntax:dict[key]=value

 #update()
-this work when two dict are there or to add new key to dict and if
 in first dict if the key is present and on new dict same key with new
 value will be updated
-Syntax:dict1.update(dict2)

#pop()
-to remove the item from
-Syntax:dict.pop(key)

#Working with keys and values
Not allowed keys-list,sets,dict =>mutable datatypes
Allowed keys-str,int,float,boolean,tuple =>immutable datatypes
*keys of a dict can only immutable datatypes
*values can be any datatype

#To fetch only the keys of dict
-keys()
-syntax:dict.key()

#To fetch only value
-value()
-syntax:dict.value()

#Item to fetch both key and value in pairs
-item()
-syntax:dict.item()

#Shallow copy
-works on mutable datatypes
-first import copy
-makes a copy in new memory location
-it do not work in inner list/ inner location in list
-Syntax:variable2=copy.copy(variable1)

#Deep copy
-works on mutable datatypes
-first import copy
-makes a copy in new memory location
-it work in inner list/ inner location in list
-Syntax:variable2=copy.deepcopy(variable1)

